<!doctype html>
<meta charset="utf-8">
<title>Entangled Desktop third-party licenses</title>
<h1>Third-party licenses</h1>

<h2>Redistributed binaries that are not Rust crates</h2>
<p>
  <code>cargo about</code> reads the Cargo dependency graph, so it cannot see
  anything we ship that is not a crate. Today that is exactly one thing:
</p>
<ul>
  <li>
    <b>TianoCore EDK2</b> — <code>artifacts/firmware/CLOUDHV.fd</code>, the UEFI
    firmware a guest boots (<code>OvmfPkg/CloudHv/CloudHvX64</code>, built by
    <code>guest/firmware/build-cloudhv.sh</code>). Licensed
    <b>BSD-2-Clause-Patent</b>. It is guest-side content: never linked into a
    host binary, only loaded into guest memory at runtime — but it <i>is</i>
    redistributed, inside the Windows installer and as a release asset, so it is
    attributed. The full notice and licence text are in
    <code>THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt</code> at the root of this repository, which
    the installer both shows on its licence page and lays down beside the
    binaries.
  </li>
</ul>
<p>
  The Linux bootstrap kernel is deliberately absent from that list: it is
  GPL-2.0-only and is <i>not</i> shipped in the installer, only published as its
  own release alongside its corresponding source
  (<code>.github/workflows/guest-artifacts.yml</code>).
</p>

<h2>Rust dependencies</h2>
{{#each licenses}}
<h3>{{name}} ({{id}})</h3>
<ul>{{#each used_by}}<li>{{crate.name}} {{crate.version}}</li>{{/each}}</ul>
<pre>{{text}}</pre>
{{/each}}
